  -f body='## Description

`pkg/apierror/apierror.go` hardcodes both the `Retry-After` header value and the user-facing message when an open-circuit error reaches the response writer:

```go
if errors.Is(err, resilience.ErrCircuitOpen) {
    c.Header("Retry-After", "30")
    c.JSON(http.StatusServiceUnavailable, &AppError{
        Code:    http.StatusServiceUnavailable,
        Message: "Service Unavailable",
        Detail:  "circuit breaker open; please retry after 30 seconds",
    })
}
```

But `internal/resilience/policy.go::Policy.BreakerCooldown` is configurable via `RAVEN_AI_WORKER_BREAKER_COOLDOWN` (default 30s). The 30s number agrees with the default — it does not track the configured value.

## Impact

- Operator sets `RAVEN_AI_WORKER_BREAKER_COOLDOWN=10s` (low-latency edge deployment) → clients wait 3× too long because `Retry-After: 30` overstates the wait.
- Operator sets `RAVEN_AI_WORKER_BREAKER_COOLDOWN=120s` (flakier upstream) → clients hammer the API up to 4× before the breaker actually closes, because `Retry-After: 30` understates the wait.
- The body text leaks the implementation detail `"circuit breaker"` to API consumers; they do not need to know we use Sony gobreaker.

A future second breaker (LiveKit, SeaweedFS, Razorpay — per the deferred HTTP audit) will compound the problem: each breaker will have its own cooldown but only one hardcoded `Retry-After` value can ever be emitted.

## Proposed Fix

**Make `ErrCircuitOpen` a typed error that carries the policy''s cooldown**, then read it in `apierror.ErrorHandler`. Keeps the error self-describing — no smuggling state through `context.Value` — and scales cleanly to multiple breakers.

```go
// internal/resilience/breaker.go
type CircuitOpenError struct {
    PolicyName string
    RetryAfter time.Duration
}

func (e *CircuitOpenError) Error() string {
    return fmt.Sprintf("resilience: circuit breaker %q open", e.PolicyName)
}

// errors.Is(err, ErrCircuitOpen) still works via:
var ErrCircuitOpen = &CircuitOpenError{}

func (e *CircuitOpenError) Is(target error) bool {
    _, ok := target.(*CircuitOpenError)
    return ok
}

// Inside Breaker.Execute, wrap with the policy''s cooldown:
case errors.Is(err, gobreaker.ErrOpenState),
     errors.Is(err, gobreaker.ErrTooManyRequests):
    return nil, &CircuitOpenError{PolicyName: b.policyName, RetryAfter: b.cooldown}
```

The `Breaker` therefore needs to remember its `policyName` and `cooldown`. Trivial: pass them through `NewBreaker` (which already takes `*Policy`).

```go
// pkg/apierror/apierror.go
var cOpen *resilience.CircuitOpenError
if errors.As(err, &cOpen) {
    retryAfter := int(cOpen.RetryAfter.Round(time.Second).Seconds())
    if retryAfter < 1 {
        retryAfter = 1
    }
    c.Header("Retry-After", strconv.Itoa(retryAfter))
    c.JSON(http.StatusServiceUnavailable, &AppError{
        Code:    http.StatusServiceUnavailable,
        Message: "Service Unavailable",
        Detail:  fmt.Sprintf("upstream service temporarily unavailable; retry after %ds", retryAfter),
    })
    return
}
```

Note the `"upstream service temporarily unavailable"` wording — replaces the implementation-leaking `"circuit breaker open"` phrase.

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] `CircuitOpenError` typed; `ErrCircuitOpen` sentinel kept for backward-compatible `errors.Is` calls.
- [ ] `Breaker` stores `policyName` + `cooldown` and uses them when constructing the error.
- [ ] `apierror.ErrorHandler` uses `errors.As` to read `RetryAfter` and emits the matching header.
- [ ] Detail message no longer contains the literal string `"circuit breaker"`.
- [ ] Test: with `RAVEN_AI_WORKER_BREAKER_COOLDOWN=12s`, the 503 response carries `Retry-After: 12`.

## References

- Files: `internal/resilience/breaker.go`, `pkg/apierror/apierror.go`, `cmd/api/main.go` (constructs the policy and breaker).
- Related: #504 (data race in same file). Pair-fix candidate, but worth a separate PR.' \
